{
  "case_reason": "The counterexample relies on the premise that 'pure water at high altitude' constitutes a valid instance of the subject 'pure water at one atmosphere'. This premise is factually false because high altitude inherently implies lower atmospheric pressure, meaning the condition 'at one atmosphere' is not met. Since the counterexample violates an explicit condition of the assertion rather than demonstrating a failure under that specific condition, it does not refute the claim. The logical structure holds: if P (one atmosphere) then Q (boils at 100C). A case where not-P occurs (high altitude) and therefore not-Q occurs (boils below 100C) is consistent with the implication and does not invalidate it.",
  "case_is_supported": true,
  "refutation_reason": "The counterexample fails to refute the assertion because it describes a scenario outside the explicit conditions of the claim. The assertion is conditional: 'pure water at one atmosphere'. The counterexample presents 'pure water at high altitude' (lower pressure). In formal logic, a conditional statement is not refuted by showing that the consequent does not hold when the antecedent is false. The case attempts to use a different physical condition (low pressure) to deny the relationship defined for a specific condition (one atmosphere), which is logically invalid. Therefore, the objection is invalid as it misapplies the scope of the original assertion.",
  "refutes_exact_assertion": false
}
